**Read:** [IAPWS-95 — fundamental water properties](../sources/IAPWS-95-Fundamental-Water-Formulation.lingua#r_4JH7YVKGMCC4R7MK1F5S1XY90Z), complete release and check values; [IAPWS-IF97 — industrial water and steam](../sources/IAPWS-IF97-Industrial-Water-and-Steam.lingua#r_7THV30AN6Q693AJKH3W6ZSG13Y), complete applicable regional release; [CoolProp — thermophysical property models and methods](../sources/CoolProp-Thermophysical-Property-Methods.lingua#r_2QYM459W81KPNJK37RHJ6VB9R1), full relevant backend, input-pair, derivative and reference-state documentation; [Open thermophysical property evaluation — Bell and colleagues](../sources/Open-Thermophysical-Library-Bell-and-Colleagues.lingua#r_33XEMZDJ2BMYZ0GDFGWDTV1C95), the entire methods paper.

Select a declared single-phase and coexistence range. Read the complete relevant IAPWS releases and identify their independent variables, regional equations, validity domains, numerical checks and stated uncertainties. Distinguish thermodynamic properties from separately modeled viscosity, conductivity and surface tension.

Compute a documented set of states with pinned software and backend, then reproduce selected release check values independently. Examine units, reference states, saturation selection, derivative definitions and branch choice. Compare IAPWS-95 with IF97 as related formulations with different purposes, not as two wholly independent experiments.

Use experimental or evaluated evidence to assess physical accuracy where it is available. A code-to-code agreement can establish implementation consistency while sharing the same physical formulation. Preserve the input pairs, fluid definition, output status and failed/out-of-range cases. Report uncertainty near critical or phase boundaries with the actual formulation's qualifications.

